1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,571
I think these dashboards are beautiful.

2
00:00:02,571 --> 00:00:05,571
The Home Assistant
community turns out cards

3
00:00:05,571 --> 00:00:08,571
that are genuinely stunning to look at.

4
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:14,143
Bar Card, one of the most widely
used cards for animated energy bars,

5
00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:18,857
lost its repository on HACS in
2025 — no official maintainer left.

6
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:22,714
The result: thousands of
dashboards with a broken red

7
00:00:22,714 --> 00:00:25,714
card, overnight, with
no warning to anyone.

8
00:00:25,714 --> 00:00:28,714
'Beautiful' and 'maintained'
are two different things.

9
00:00:28,714 --> 00:00:33,857
And there's a second problem,
and this one is more serious: if

10
00:00:33,857 --> 00:00:38,571
you want to reach that dashboard
from your phone when you're

11
00:00:38,571 --> 00:00:43,286
away from home, it has to
be exposed to the internet.

12
00:00:43,286 --> 00:00:48,000
And it isn't just cards you're
exposing — it's your personal data.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,429
Your presence patterns, your
cameras, sometimes your locks.

14
00:00:51,429 --> 00:00:54,857
A poorly secured dashboard
on a platform reachable

15
00:00:54,857 --> 00:00:58,714
from outside is an open
door into your house.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,286
Faced with those two problems —
maintenance that gives out, and

17
00:01:04,286 --> 00:01:09,857
security that gets neglected — I made
one decision up front, and it's the

18
00:01:09,857 --> 00:01:14,143
one that defines the whole
project: build something simple and

19
00:01:14,143 --> 00:01:19,286
effective, that ships with as much
security as possible by default, not

20
00:01:19,286 --> 00:01:25,286
as an option you bolt on later
if you happen to think of it.

21
00:01:25,286 --> 00:01:30,000
In practice that means: as few
external dependencies I have no

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,714
control over as possible, an
architecture designed from day one to

23
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:39,857
be exposed to the internet without
that being a gamble, and an

24
00:01:39,857 --> 00:01:43,714
environment that lets me roll
back cleanly when something

25
00:01:43,714 --> 00:01:48,429
breaks — because it will break,
sooner or later, on any project.

26
00:01:48,429 --> 00:01:52,714
Concretely, that translates
into a CI/CD setup that lets one

27
00:01:52,714 --> 00:01:56,571
self-taught person — not a DevOps
team, just someone motivated —

28
00:01:56,571 --> 00:02:01,286
grow the product step by step,
or simply restore a previous

29
00:02:01,286 --> 00:02:05,143
version straight into HAOS
when an update goes wrong.

30
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:10,286
We'll go deep on that pipeline
in block A of the series,

31
00:02:10,286 --> 00:02:14,571
and backups have a block
of their own, block B.

32
00:02:14,571 --> 00:02:20,143
But I wanted you to know, right
here in the first episode, that

33
00:02:20,143 --> 00:02:24,857
this safety net exists and that
it's part of the foundation.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,571
There's a third reason behind all
this, simpler to state but just as

35
00:02:35,571 --> 00:02:39,000
central: Home Assistant's native
interface is very complex.

36
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,714
Powerful, yes — but complex.

37
00:02:40,714 --> 00:02:45,000
Between the configuration menus,
the entities, the areas, the nested

38
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,714
dashboards, it's easy to get lost,
especially when you're starting out.

39
00:02:49,714 --> 00:02:55,286
What Visio Sapiens sets out to do is
simplify that experience — not hide

40
00:02:55,286 --> 00:02:59,571
Home Assistant's power, but make
it reachable through a coherent

41
00:02:59,571 --> 00:03:03,857
interface, designed for daily use
rather than for technical configuration.

42
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,143
So what is it, concretely?

43
00:03:32,143 --> 00:03:34,714
Visio Sapiens is not a dashboard.

44
00:03:34,714 --> 00:03:39,000
It's a control-center-style interface,
sitting on top of Home Assistant.

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,286
The distinction matters.

46
00:03:40,286 --> 00:03:44,143
A dashboard is a collection
of cards you assemble.

47
00:03:44,143 --> 00:03:48,429
A control center is a
coherent system, with its own

48
00:03:48,429 --> 00:03:52,714
rendering engine, its own visual
logic, its own identity — and

49
00:03:52,714 --> 00:03:57,000
it simply goes to Home
Assistant to fetch the data.

50
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
And that distinction has a very concrete
consequence, one you'll see at work in

51
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,286
every episode: the interface isn't
assembled by hand, it's generated.

52
00:04:07,286 --> 00:04:12,429
You describe your home once, in an
admin console — the language, the

53
00:04:12,429 --> 00:04:16,714
format, the rooms, the look —
and every screen is produced

54
00:04:16,714 --> 00:04:21,429
from that description, then kept
in step as the home changes.

55
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:25,286
Adding a room isn't drawing
a new dashboard: it's

56
00:04:25,286 --> 00:04:29,571
declaring a room, and letting
the generator do the rest.

57
00:04:29,571 --> 00:04:32,571
A dashboard you assemble by hand ages.

58
00:04:32,571 --> 00:04:35,143
A dashboard that's generated keeps up.

59
00:04:35,143 --> 00:04:40,286
And that leads to the single rule
that drives every technical decision

60
00:04:40,286 --> 00:04:45,000
in this project, the one you'll
meet again in every episode:

61
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,857
Home Assistant is nothing
more than a data engine.

62
00:04:48,857 --> 00:04:51,857
The interface is driven entirely by VSSP.

63
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,857
So here's the question that
comes up straight away:

64
00:05:03,857 --> 00:05:07,286
why not just use the
native Lovelace cards?

65
00:05:07,286 --> 00:05:10,286
They exist, they're maintained by the Home

66
00:05:10,286 --> 00:05:12,857
Assistant core, they work perfectly well.

67
00:05:12,857 --> 00:05:18,857
Exactly — they work perfectly well for
what they are: a system of stacked cards.

68
00:05:18,857 --> 00:05:24,000
But the moment you want a
coherent visual identity and a HUD

69
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,286
that behaves like a real
system interface rather than a

70
00:05:28,286 --> 00:05:31,714
grid of widgets, you hit
Lovelace's glass ceiling.

71
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:35,571
So the project made a call:
don't assemble ready-made

72
00:05:35,571 --> 00:05:39,000
cards, build a rendering
engine of its own.

73
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,714
And I want to be precise,
because the short version would

74
00:05:43,714 --> 00:05:47,143
be wrong: this engine
doesn't start from nothing.

75
00:05:47,143 --> 00:05:50,143
It stands on three
community building blocks —

76
00:05:50,143 --> 00:05:51,857
button-card, card-mod and layout-card.

77
00:05:51,857 --> 00:05:55,714
Foundations, not widgets: one
draws, one styles, one places.

78
00:05:55,714 --> 00:05:59,571
Everything you see on top —
the navigation, the header

79
00:05:59,571 --> 00:06:03,000
band, the gauges, the
rooms — is templates I

80
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
write, generated and
versioned in the repository.

81
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,571
And a handful of specialised cards
do what it would be absurd to

82
00:06:11,571 --> 00:06:15,429
rewrite: the charts, the weather,
the calendar, the history.

83
00:06:15,429 --> 00:06:20,143
The difference from a typical community
dashboard is the exposed surface.

84
00:06:20,143 --> 00:06:25,714
Three foundations and a handful of cards,
which the updates screen watches as

85
00:06:25,714 --> 00:06:30,429
a family of their own — not thirty
widgets with thirty maintainers.

86
00:06:30,429 --> 00:06:34,714
The day a building block
gives out, it's one block

87
00:06:34,714 --> 00:06:38,143
to replace, not thirty
cards to track down.

88
00:06:38,143 --> 00:06:42,857
The price of that choice is
real, let's be honest: for

89
00:06:42,857 --> 00:06:46,286
everything above the foundations,
I become the maintainer.

90
00:06:46,286 --> 00:06:51,000
But that cost is one
I control: a bug in my

91
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
templates, I fix it that same evening.

92
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,143
And card-mod deserves a word.

93
00:06:56,143 --> 00:06:59,143
On my own templates, it's a tool.

94
00:06:59,143 --> 00:07:05,143
Inside the shadow DOM of a card I
don't control, it's a patch — and

95
00:07:05,143 --> 00:07:09,857
I still have one: to force
the language of a third-party

96
00:07:09,857 --> 00:07:15,000
weather card, I hide its text
and write another one over it.

97
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,857
It holds until the update
that renames a class.

98
00:07:18,857 --> 00:07:24,429
That's why that kind of patch stays
an exception, and why it's documented.

99
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Here's what the full
architecture looks like.

100
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,714
I'm showing it to you once,
in full, because it's the

101
00:07:37,714 --> 00:07:41,143
map you'll need to
follow the whole series.

102
00:07:41,143 --> 00:07:45,000
Five places hold something, and
they are not interchangeable.

103
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,857
The Git repository, on my
workstation: the model, the

104
00:07:48,857 --> 00:07:51,000
templates, the translations, the tooling.

105
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,429
The GitLab pipeline, which
validates, builds and deploys.

106
00:07:54,429 --> 00:07:58,286
Two targets, receiving the
same package: a k3s cluster

107
00:07:58,286 --> 00:08:01,286
for staging, Home Assistant
OS for production.

108
00:08:01,286 --> 00:08:06,000
And the browser — the tablet, the
phone — where the dashboards appear.

109
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,429
In the middle, one single
command: the generator.

110
00:08:09,429 --> 00:08:14,143
It takes the description of the
house, the templates and the

111
00:08:14,143 --> 00:08:18,000
language, and it produces
every dashboard and the theme.

112
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,571
Nobody writes a dashboard by hand.

113
00:08:20,571 --> 00:08:25,714
And every screen of the admin
console follows the same loop: a

114
00:08:25,714 --> 00:08:30,000
form sends what you decided,
a script backs up first,

115
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,857
writes the model, regenerates,
then reports what it did.

116
00:08:33,857 --> 00:08:39,000
Take one thing away from this
diagram: every piece has one precise

117
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,857
responsibility, and none of
them does another one's job.

118
00:08:42,857 --> 00:08:49,714
That's what lets the project hold up over
time instead of turning into a plate of

119
00:08:49,714 --> 00:08:56,143
YAML spaghetti — and it's what makes the
rest of the series possible: you can only

120
00:08:56,143 --> 00:09:01,714
version, back up and restore cleanly
what is structured in the first place.

121
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Enough theory, let's look at the result.

122
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,571
This is HOME, the landing screen, as
it appears on the living-room tablet.

123
00:09:38,571 --> 00:09:42,429
On the left, the navigation
rail: one entry per

124
00:09:42,429 --> 00:09:45,000
declared room, plus the system screens.

125
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:51,429
It's generated too — add a room, and its
entry appears on every screen at once.

126
00:09:51,429 --> 00:09:55,286
At the top, the header
band: the screen's name

127
00:09:55,286 --> 00:09:58,286
and the time, the weather, the calendar.

128
00:09:58,286 --> 00:10:03,857
The same band on every dashboard,
because it comes from a single template.

129
00:10:03,857 --> 00:10:10,286
Just below, the status row: the system,
the energy, the alarm with its modes and

130
00:10:10,286 --> 00:10:15,429
its panic button, the thermostat — and
this updates light, which turns orange

131
00:10:15,429 --> 00:10:21,000
when something is waiting, and leads
to the screen that says exactly what.

132
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,286
In the centre, the protocol
radar, and next to it,

133
00:10:25,286 --> 00:10:28,286
the metered devices with what they draw.

134
00:10:28,286 --> 00:10:32,143
Then the assistant bar, and
the house's latest events.

135
00:10:32,143 --> 00:10:37,714
And now the part I most enjoy
showing, because it makes all of

136
00:10:37,714 --> 00:10:41,143
this concrete: where this
screen's look comes from.

137
00:10:41,143 --> 00:10:45,429
On the left, a file in the
repository, design_system.yaml: every

138
00:10:45,429 --> 00:10:49,714
colour, the typeface, the
size of each kind of text.

139
00:10:49,714 --> 00:10:53,571
On the right, the console
screen that edits it.

140
00:10:53,571 --> 00:10:57,857
I raise the size of the
values — and every screen

141
00:10:57,857 --> 00:11:01,286
follows at once, because
there's only one source.

142
00:11:01,286 --> 00:11:07,286
Not ten cards to touch up one by
one, hoping you didn't miss any.

143
00:11:07,286 --> 00:11:12,000
The code and the interface
speak the same language — and so

144
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
they're versioned like
a real software project.

145
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,571
I told you the interface is generated
from a description of the house.

146
00:11:35,571 --> 00:11:39,000
Here's where you write
it: the admin console.

147
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,143
Nine screens, one per decision.

148
00:11:41,143 --> 00:11:43,286
The rooms and the floors.

149
00:11:43,286 --> 00:11:46,286
The devices the network
has just discovered.

150
00:11:46,286 --> 00:11:48,857
Which room each device belongs to.

151
00:11:48,857 --> 00:11:51,857
The Google calendar,
the visual charter, the

152
00:11:51,857 --> 00:11:54,857
dashboards themselves,
the updates, and the safe.

153
00:11:54,857 --> 00:11:57,000
Let's declare a room, live.

154
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
A laundry room, on the ground floor.

155
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:00,857
I apply.

156
00:12:00,857 --> 00:12:07,286
What happens during those few seconds
is the loop I showed you on the diagram.

157
00:12:07,286 --> 00:12:09,857
The form sends what I decided.

158
00:12:09,857 --> 00:12:15,000
A script backs up the current state
first — always, before writing anything.

159
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,857
It writes the model, reruns
the generator, and reports

160
00:12:18,857 --> 00:12:22,286
back: that message, up
there, is the report.

161
00:12:22,286 --> 00:12:24,429
I go back to HOME.

162
00:12:24,429 --> 00:12:27,000
The laundry is in the rail.

163
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:32,571
And it's there on every screen at
once — the energy, the system, the

164
00:12:32,571 --> 00:12:36,429
console — because the rail
isn't written by hand anywhere.

165
00:12:36,429 --> 00:12:41,571
One honest detail: the room's own
screen is declared in Home Assistant's

166
00:12:41,571 --> 00:12:45,857
configuration, and Home Assistant only
rereads that configuration at startup.

167
00:12:45,857 --> 00:12:50,571
Its entry appears straight away;
its screen, at the next restart.

168
00:12:50,571 --> 00:12:54,857
I'd rather tell you
than cut it in the edit.

169
00:12:54,857 --> 00:12:58,286
And it's the same gesture
for everything else.

170
00:12:58,286 --> 00:13:00,429
You never draw a dashboard.

171
00:13:00,429 --> 00:13:03,000
You decide, and the console writes.

172
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,857
At the start I promised
you security by default.

173
00:14:03,857 --> 00:14:08,143
A promise can be checked —
so here's what it means

174
00:14:08,143 --> 00:14:10,714
concretely, including what isn't done yet.

175
00:14:10,714 --> 00:14:14,571
One: the console's forms
send nothing with a password.

176
00:14:14,571 --> 00:14:17,571
They go through entry
points Home Assistant

177
00:14:17,571 --> 00:14:20,143
only accepts from the local network.

178
00:14:20,143 --> 00:14:22,714
From the internet, they don't exist.

179
00:14:22,714 --> 00:14:26,143
Two: a new tablet keeps
no permanent credential.

180
00:14:26,143 --> 00:14:30,857
The console screens borrow the
session you logged in with, a

181
00:14:30,857 --> 00:14:33,857
short-lived token Home
Assistant renews by itself.

182
00:14:33,857 --> 00:14:36,857
Losing the tablet isn't losing a key.

183
00:14:36,857 --> 00:14:40,714
Three: secrets live in
a safe, not in files.

184
00:14:40,714 --> 00:14:46,714
Home Assistant can read the name of every
secret there, never its value — because

185
00:14:46,714 --> 00:14:51,857
everything Home Assistant reads ends
up in clear in its history database.

186
00:14:51,857 --> 00:14:56,571
The safe closes at every
restart, and you reopen it with

187
00:14:56,571 --> 00:14:59,571
one passphrase, from a
device you've enrolled.

188
00:14:59,571 --> 00:15:04,714
Four: no password ever goes
through a command line or into a

189
00:15:04,714 --> 00:15:08,143
log — not the router's,
not the assistants' keys.

190
00:15:08,143 --> 00:15:10,286
And now, what isn't done.

191
00:15:10,286 --> 00:15:15,857
Today, on the local network, the Home
Assistant password still travels in clear.

192
00:15:15,857 --> 00:15:21,429
The move to HTTPS is written and
documented; it isn't in place yet.

193
00:15:21,429 --> 00:15:24,857
It has its own episode, in block A.

194
00:15:24,857 --> 00:15:30,000
That's what security by default
means to me: not a list of

195
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,714
promises, but a list of things
you can check — and, published

196
00:15:34,714 --> 00:15:39,000
right beside it, the list
of the ones still missing.

197
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,000
A quick aside before we wrap up,
because you're going to run into this:

198
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,286
the project was originally called
OSVision, before it became VSSP.

199
00:16:40,286 --> 00:16:45,429
In September, a big sweep removed
the old name from the identifiers,

200
00:16:45,429 --> 00:16:49,286
the components and the stylesheets —
and that sweep surfaced

201
00:16:49,286 --> 00:16:53,571
three real bugs, including a
dead link nobody had noticed.

202
00:16:53,571 --> 00:16:59,571
But some traces remain: the JavaScript
engine still carries the old name, and the

203
00:16:59,571 --> 00:17:06,000
K3s panel on the CORE screen still
looks for its file under the old path.

204
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:10,286
A good project doesn't hide
those marks, it documents them.

205
00:17:10,286 --> 00:17:13,714
That one, we'll fix live in episode D1.

206
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,714
None of what you've just seen
existed a few months ago.

207
00:17:19,714 --> 00:17:22,286
A generator that produces every screen.

208
00:17:22,286 --> 00:17:24,857
A console that declares the rooms.

209
00:17:24,857 --> 00:17:28,286
A safe that unseals
from an enrolled device.

210
00:17:28,286 --> 00:17:32,143
An updates screen that
knows what it doesn't know.

211
00:17:32,143 --> 00:17:36,429
A visual charter that changes
right down to the typeface.

212
00:17:36,429 --> 00:17:40,286
This isn't a frozen
project — it's a system with

213
00:17:40,286 --> 00:17:43,286
a history, and it's still writing one.

214
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,857
The rest of the series
is organised into four

215
00:18:03,857 --> 00:18:06,857
blocks, and you choose your way in.

216
00:18:06,857 --> 00:18:12,429
Every episode runs about twenty
minutes: time to show, not just to tell.

217
00:18:12,429 --> 00:18:16,714
Block A is the foundation:
the machine, the cluster, the

218
00:18:16,714 --> 00:18:21,000
pipeline, and what breaks
when one of the three lies.

219
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:27,429
Block B is backup — all the way to the
only proof that counts: the restore.

220
00:18:27,429 --> 00:18:32,571
Block C is the safe: where
the secrets live, and how you

221
00:18:32,571 --> 00:18:36,000
unseal it without typing
three keys by hand.

222
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,571
And block D is the interface itself,
from the gauge to the generator.

223
00:18:41,571 --> 00:18:44,143
Inside a block, the order matters.

224
00:18:44,143 --> 00:18:48,857
Between blocks, it doesn't: if
you came for the backups, you

225
00:18:48,857 --> 00:18:52,714
don't have to sit through
ten interface episodes first.

226
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,714
What to take away: Visio Sapiens
isn't a dashboard, it's a

227
00:19:04,714 --> 00:19:08,143
control center, simple,
